+# Prompt Batch Layout
+
+This capsule owns the text prompt contribution to a llama.cpp scheduler batch in single-slot serving. The planner supplies the pinned engine, scope and token budget from its contract and ontology. The runtime reuses the stock queue, splitting, checkpoint and output paths. The cap applies to splittable causal text completions without embeddings or a loaded multimodal projector. Multi-slot serving bypasses the policy. The earlier Satay prototype activated the policy in multi-slot serving and reproducibly changed the long request's greedy output.
+
+Call `plan` with `engine_commit`, `scope` (`single-slot-causal-text-completion`), `parallel` (1), `n_batch`, `n_ubatch` and `prompt_cap`. Use the returned arguments and environment, and apply the sealed `server_patch` material to the exact pinned stock source. Compare its SHA-256 with the returned policy before applying. Run the candidate with the variable unset to retain the stock scheduling path. The enabled path logs functional engagement per slot.
+
+Call `verify` with the same spec and uncached fixture records drawn from independent stock and candidate runs. Each record identifies the fixture, prompt token count, ordered cap engagement rows, and each arm's generated token IDs and stop reason. Include at least one fixture longer than the cap. Retain the raw request, response and server log. If mixed workloads or reused prefixes are part of the proposed test, isolate and account for the newly processed tokens per slot before adding their fixture requirements.
+
+The Satay pilot checks integration and functional equivalence on a small routed model. Its test plan carries no throughput or DGX Spark performance gate. A Spark evaluation needs its own source applicability check and matched stock comparisons, including stock batch and microbatch settings.
